---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-11-24';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Editorial policy', path: '/editorial-policy/' },
  ]}
  description="What singletenant.ai counts as single-tenant, how vendor facts are sourced and re-checked, how AI is used to make the site, and how corrections and conflicts are handled."
>
  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Editorial</p>
      <h1 class="m-0 mb-2" style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Editorial policy
      </h1>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs mb-10" style="color: var(--color-mute);">
        Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This policy covers everything published on singletenant.ai: the
          vendor database, the comparisons generated from it, and the guides
          and analysis. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page
          describes how the database is built; this page sets out the rules
          the editor holds it to.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-qualifies">What counts as single-tenant</h2>
        <p>
          "Dedicated" and "single-tenant" are used loosely in vendor
          marketing, so this site separates the layers at which a service can
          be kept apart from other customers. From the least to the most
          isolated:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Dedicated GPUs.</strong> The accelerators serving your
            model are reserved for you, but the host, network and control
            plane may be shared with other customers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dedicated nodes.</strong> Whole servers (or bare-metal
            nodes) run only your workload, so no other customer's code runs on
            the same machine.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Isolated network.</strong> Your deployment sits in its own
            network segment, with traffic to and from it controlled, for
            example by private endpoints or egress allow-lists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Customer VPC.</strong> The service runs inside a cloud
            account or virtual private cloud that you control, with the
            vendor operating the software.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>On-premises.</strong> The software runs on hardware you
            own or lease, in a data centre you choose, with no vendor-operated
            infrastructure in the data path.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          A vendor qualifies for the database when it publicly documents
          inference capacity reserved for one customer rather than pooled.
          Most qualify at the first level or above. The hyperscalers'
          provisioned throughput products (the "hyperscaler dedicated
          capacity" category) are included because the capacity is reserved
          for one customer, but they sit below the first level: they reserve
          throughput, and the vendors do not say which hardware serves it.
          Shared per-token APIs alone do not qualify. Each level is a claim
          the vendor has to document; the site does not infer a level from
          pricing, branding or a sales conversation. The vendor profiles do
          not yet record a level per vendor; when they do, it will be added
          to every profile at once, each with its own source.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sourcing">Sourcing</h2>
        <p>
          Vendor facts come from primary sources: the vendor's own
          documentation, trust centre, legal terms, contracts published on its
          site, and pricing pages. Regulatory and legal statements in guides
          and analysis come from the regulator, the legislation, or the
          official publication itself. Press coverage, analyst summaries,
          forum threads, marketing listicles, and AI-generated summaries are
          not sources, though they can prompt a check of the primary material.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every vendor profile lists, field by field, the primary source each
          field was checked against and the date of that check, and every
          populated fact links to the specific page behind it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verification">Verification</h2>
        <p>
          A fact is recorded as verified only when the linked primary source
          states it on the date shown. If the source is ambiguous, only implies
          the claim, or cannot be reached, the fact is recorded as "Not
          verified". It is never inferred from a related claim, a launch blog,
          or a rebrand.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each vendor profile shows a "Last verified" date: the date of the
          oldest check behind any field on the page, so the headline date never
          overstates how fresh the profile is. Individual sections carry their
          own dates. Comparison pages show the older of the two vendors' dates.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cadence">How often things are checked</h2>
        <p>
          Every week, each vendor's own changelog, pricing, documentation and
          trust pages are checked for changes, and a change goes on the site
          only after its primary source has been read. Separately, a few
          profiles are re-checked in full each week, field by field. Every
          field on every profile is re-checked at least every 60 days; the
          target from January 2027 is every six weeks. A profile's "Last
          verified" date moves only when every field on it has been
          re-checked, so a partial check never makes a profile look fresher
          than it is.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-made">How this site is made</h2>
        <p>
          The site is researched and drafted with AI tools (Claude, made by
          Anthropic), and every claim is checked against a dated primary
          source before it is published. The same rule covers the parts
          readers rarely see: page titles, search descriptions, structured
          data and image alt text. The AI agent works to written rules set by
          the editor, Simon Newton, who is accountable for everything
          published.
        </p>
        <p>
          Simon reviews the site's changes in a weekly digest. Routine changes
          (a re-sourced fact, a broken link, a correction) go live once the
          automated checks pass and are reviewed in that digest. Larger
          changes, such as a new vendor, a new page, or a change to this
          policy or the methodology, are held for 48 hours before
          publication so that they can be reviewed or stopped first. Every
          change is kept in version history and can be reversed, and every
          change to the data is listed in the public
          <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The automated checks refuse a vendor fact without a source link and
          a date, refuse an edited fact whose source was not re-checked, and
          cap analyst notes at 60 words. No page is generated in bulk without
          its own sourced data behind it: comparison pages are offered to search
          engines only when the two vendors differ on enough verified facts,
          or when the pair has its own written, sourced analysis.
        </p>

        <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Anyone can request a correction, vendors included, by emailing
          <a href="mailto:simon@singletenant.ai">simon@singletenant.ai</a>
          with the page, the claim, and a link to the primary source that
          contradicts it. The claim is re-checked against the vendor's
          published material. If it fails, the data is corrected, its verified
          date is updated, and the correction is logged as a dated entry in the
          <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> and on the
          <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>. Corrections are never made
          silently, and a correction request is not accepted without a primary
          source, whoever sends it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Errors in guides and analysis are corrected in place, with the
          article's updated date changed and, for any substantive error, a
          note in the text saying what changed.
        </p>

        <h2 id="conflicts">Conflicts of interest</h2>
        <p>
          No vendor can pay for inclusion, placement, ordering, or the content
          of its profile or comparisons. Commercial relationships (referral
          links, advertising, and sponsorship) are kept separate from the data
          and are listed on the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a>.
          Paid placements are always labelled where they appear.
        </p>
        <p>
          The editor does not sell consulting or other services through this
          site. If the editor has a financial relationship with a vendor
          covered here, it is listed on the disclosure page, and that vendor's
          profile is not edited until the relationship is disclosed.
        </p>
        <p>
          At present there is one: Simon holds a RunPod referral account. The
          site carries no referral links, advertising or sponsorship, and
          RunPod's profile is held to the same sourcing rules as every other.
        </p>
        <p>
          Vendors do not review or approve copy before publication. A vendor
          may point out a factual error after publication, through the same
          corrections process as any reader.
        </p>

        <h2 id="ai-use">How AI systems may use this site</h2>
        <p>
          The site's <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a> carries a Content
          Signal: <code>search=yes, ai-input=yes, ai-train=no</code>. Search
          engines may index the site and show it in results. AI systems may
          read pages to answer a question as it is asked, for example in an AI
          search answer or a retrieval tool, ideally linking back to the
          source page. We do not consent to the content being used to train or
          fine-tune AI models. The signal states a preference; it is not a
          technical block.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
